00:05:26,470 --> 00:05:28,190
How many vocab elements should I have?

76
00:05:28,190 --> 00:05:34,910
and then after that, we're going to talk about very low-level tricks of how to get models to train stably.

77
00:05:34,910 --> 00:05:44,310
And the reason why I'm going to talk about that in this lecture is because these stability tricks have a pretty close connection with the architecture variation, right?

78
00:05:44,310 --> 00:05:51,790
one of the things that, you know, higher level I want to sort of impress upon you is that architectures are actually a a very complex set of tradeoffs, right?

79
00:05:51,790 --> 00:05:53,840
Like what does a architecture have to do?

80
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:57,270
Well, it has to learn from data, so it has to generalize.

81
00:05:57,270 --> 00:06:00,270
It has to train efficiently on GPUs.

82
00:06:00,270 --> 00:06:01,350
And it has to not blow up, right?

83
00:06:01,350 --> 00:06:08,790
Like halfway through training, if your, you know, training losses just go like down like this and then suddenly blow up, that's no good at all, right?

84
00:06:08,790 --> 00:06:12,230
So all these different requirements end up getting baked straight into the architecture.

85
00:06:12,230 --> 00:06:15,960
And that's why these things are a little bit messy and a little bit complex.

86
00:06:15,960 --> 00:06:21,040
but you should keep that in mind and that's why, you know, things aren't in many ways not so elegant.

87
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:22,280
Okay.

88
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:26,950
So we're going to start with the core architecture piece.

89
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:41,790
And as a high-level view, you know, I think the the way that I see a lot of the architecture stuff, you know, looking basically historically, is kind of in the early days of, you know, starting with the transformer until, you know, GPT-3 or so, there's a lot of experimentation that happens.

90
00:06:41,790 --> 00:06:43,670
People try lots of different things.

91
00:06:43,670 --> 00:06:45,880
There's no like gold standard that everyone has unified on.

92
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:51,590
And then, you know, LLaMA 2 comes out and everyone's like, wow, LLaMA 2 is great. I want my own LLaMA 2.

93
00:06:51,590 --> 00:06:58,950
And so everyone starts training LLaMA 2-alikes with, you know, minor variation that people have.

94
00:06:58,950 --> 00:07:07,790
And then finally, you know, last year we saw really big differences or or sort of a trend towards architecture modifications that make training more stable.

95
00:07:07,790 --> 00:07:14,630
And this year we see lots of trends towards architecture variations that enable longer context dependence.

96
00:07:14,630 --> 00:07:21,990
So there are these big themes that are happening, but really I think, you know, you see this like big point when LLaMA 2 comes out and everyone's like, wow, I want to train something with that.

97
00:07:21,990 --> 00:07:27,110
And then suddenly, or not suddenly, but after that, people are starting to explore once again.

98
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:29,640
So it's kind of cool to see all these different changes.

99
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:30,560
Okay.

100
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:46,470
I think people can disagree about a lot of things on architectures, but there is one thing that everyone agrees on, you know, like if you take the transformer paper, I think a lot of people will say like the transformer people got like most of the things right, except this.

101
00:07:46,470 --> 00:07:55,910
And the thing that they really did not get right or like I think most people agree they did not get right is where you put the layer norm, right?

102
00:07:55,910 --> 00:08:01,310
So in the original um uh transformer paper, you know, the layer norm goes in what you would call the residual path, right?

103
00:08:01,310 --> 00:08:11,750
So in the transformer, you know, you have the residual stream, this X that kind of runs through the whole network.

104
00:08:11,750 --> 00:08:15,600
And sort of a delta back into this residual stream.

105
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:24,920
And then in order to make sure that these gradients are sort of stable across layers, you know, a layer norm is placed at the end of each of these components.

106
00:08:24,920 --> 00:08:32,110
Now, um instead of putting the layer norms in the residual stream, there's an alternative.

107
00:08:32,110 --> 00:08:40,110
I'll refer to this as pre-norm, um in which you can put the layer norm outside of the residual stream, but before each of the computations.

108
00:08:40,110 --> 00:08:45,600
So you can put it before the multi-head attention, you can put it before the FFN, right? Um we'll call this pre-norm.

109
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:50,390
the nomenclature will get a little bit confusing.

110
00:08:50,390 --> 00:08:53,830
You can call this uh post-norm for now, but let's call this sort of residual norm, right?

111
00:08:53,830 --> 00:08:56,240
Cuz you're putting the norm in the residual layer.

112
00:08:56,240 --> 00:09:03,590
basically all modern language models uh push the layer norm outside of the residual stream.

113
00:09:03,590 --> 00:09:06,560
This is just like a thing that basically everybody does.

114
00:09:06,560 --> 00:09:11,400
there is one funny exception, but it is OPT-350M.

115
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:21,310
And if you all are familiar with sort of language models, we kind of know OPT in general was, you know, kind of a mess of a language model, right?

116
00:09:21,310 --> 00:09:28,920
And OPT-350M um is even more so because I don't know why only that model uh has a post layer norm in the residual stream.

117
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:32,790
So this is one of the things that like everyone agrees on.

118
00:09:32,790 --> 00:09:42,280
And so you might wonder like why is this like such a, you know, uh like a uni- unified thing across all the different models?

119
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:56,830
and if you look at some of the early works studying like where do you place the layer norm style research, um what you really see is that, you know, the early motivation for a lot of this was when you train a transformer, you need

120
00:09:56,830 --> 00:09:58,470
when you train a transformer, you need to do a warm-up.

121
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:02,270
Actually, you know, modern transformer training still does warm-ups as well.

122
00:10:02,270 --> 00:10:04,560
But you definitely need to do warm-up when you train.

123
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:08,270
Now, wouldn't it be nice if we could remove the warm-up, right?

124
00:10:08,270 --> 00:10:11,320
So, that was kind of the initial motivation for a lot of this research.

125
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:20,230
But people quickly realized that removing the warm-up had very serious issues in terms of the stability and convergence of these things, right?

126
00:10:20,230 --> 00:10:25,840
So, if you did post norm plus layer norm, which is, you know, basically the original transformer thing, you got this purple dash line.

127
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:32,920
Oh, you just don't converge as well compared to doing something like pre norm. You can ignore the other terms.

128
00:10:32,920 --> 00:10:36,150
You would get much nicer convergence even without warm-up, right?

129
00:10:36,150 --> 00:10:38,640
So, this was the original motivation.

130
00:10:38,640 --> 00:10:52,750
But really what people kind of quickly realized is that, you know, moving the layer norms outside the residual stream has some pretty important implications as you make your network deeper and as you start to grapple with stability

131
00:10:52,750 --> 00:10:54,400
you start to grapple with stability issues.

132
00:10:54,400 --> 00:11:00,280
To me, I think the gradient attenuation issues are kind of the most clear.

133
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:07,710
When you sort of talk to people who do architecture design, I'm not really one of the the people that deeply engages in this.

134
00:11:07,710 --> 00:11:12,030
But one of the things that people often say is keep your residual stream clean, right?

135
00:11:12,030 --> 00:11:17,910
So, in this case, you have your X's coming in from the bottom on the pre norm side, and this X propagates all the way up to the top, right?

136
00:11:17,910 --> 00:11:20,040
All the way up to your your final output.

137
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:25,600
And that allows gradients to propagate if you in the backward pass straight through this, right?

138
00:11:25,600 --> 00:11:33,310
that makes gradient propagation very simple, which improves both stability and signal propagation.

139
00:11:33,310 --> 00:11:43,600
And that's sort of what people realized very, very quickly, that if you do something like pre norm in blue initialization, sort of the gradient size is kind of remains the same, right?

140
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:48,350
Because you have this nice straight through propagation in the backward pass.

141
00:11:48,350 --> 00:11:56,670
On the other hand, if you have post layer norm, you have these kind of complicated effects that happen because you're layer norming each time you're going through a transformer block.

142
00:11:56,670 --> 00:12:00,720
And that's going to change the norm of your gradients as you go backwards through.

143
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:09,400
So, you can kind of see, you know, from the principle of keep your residual stream clean, that pre norm makes a lot of sense.

144
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:22,710
People also realized through experimentation that this also improves stability in general, that the sizes and frequencies of gradient spikes were improved under pre norm compared to post norm.

145
00:12:22,710 --> 00:12:28,760
And, you know, this is a figure from Salazar and UN, who were one of the first ones, I think, to to study this phenomena carefully.

146
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:33,110
I think this is the reason why it stuck around, right?

147
00:12:33,110 --> 00:12:37,910
Stability and the ability to go deep are both very, very important for modern large language models.

148
00:12:37,910 --> 00:12:45,480
And so, this idea of moving your layer norm outside of the residual stream is one that basically everyone has adopted.

149
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:55,790
so now, you know, if putting layer norms in residual streams is bad, why does layer norm have to be at the start?

150
00:12:55,790 --> 00:12:59,950
Of course, we have pre norm, which is, you know, before our computation, but we could have it after computation as well, right?

151
00:12:59,950 --> 00:13:02,960
That's equally good at least under that knowledge logic.

152
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:10,150
and that's exactly right.

153
00:13:10,150 --> 00:13:13,910
Many recent models like Grok or Gemma 2 or Olmo 2 have the structure where they moved the layer norm after the computation.

154
00:13:13,910 --> 00:13:17,400
So, it's a post norm of a kind, but it's outside the residual stream.

155
00:13:17,400 --> 00:13:20,230
Other models still actually just put layer norms everywhere.

156
00:13:20,230 --> 00:13:23,200
They put a layer norm here, they put a layer norm after.

157
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:36,240
I'll get to this later as we talk about stability, but one of the other lessons that it seems to have held up very well is if you have stability issues, you can kind of sprinkle in layer norms everywhere, and that will generally improve stability.

158
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:39,590
It's almost very strange to be saying this cuz it's so ridiculous.

159
00:13:39,590 --> 00:13:42,990
And yet, that statement has actually been proven right.

160
00:13:42,990 --> 00:13:48,190
Every time, you know, people have encountered stability issues, they say,"Oh, but what if we just throw a layer norm into attention?"

161
00:13:48,190 --> 00:13:49,670
Turns out that works, too. We'll get to that later.

162
00:13:49,670 --> 00:13:55,080
So, okay, that's post norm or double norm in this case where you have two layer norms here.

163
00:13:55,080 --> 00:14:00,110
Okay.

164
00:14:00,110 --> 00:14:03,070
The other thing that you can do is in the original transformer, you have the layer norm, which is this operation right here.

165
00:14:03,070 --> 00:14:04,760
So, you have your activations X.

166
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:09,960
You're going to mean subtract, divide the variance, and then scale it back up, right? And this works just fine, right?

167
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:13,829
It's not like this is wrong.

168
00:14:13,829 --> 00:14:15,000
And many models have successfully trained on this scheme.

169
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:24,230
But basically, most or all modern models, I think, use RMS norm, which doesn't subtract the mean or add a bias term, right?

170
00:14:24,230 --> 00:14:27,510
So, it's just a scaling down and scaling back up, right?

171
00:14:27,510 --> 00:14:28,760
So, you can see this in the equation here.

172
00:14:28,760 --> 00:14:33,350
and really, layer norm is more expressive than RMS norm.

173
00:14:33,350 --> 00:14:38,280
So, there's really representationally no reason why you have to use RMS norm.

174
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:44,440
But RMS norm is nice because in practice, there's really no expressiveness loss.

175
00:14:44,440 --> 00:14:49,510
RMS norm models just as well as layer norm.

176
00:14:49,510 --> 00:14:51,230
But more importantly, it is, you know, faster, right?

177
00:14:51,230 --> 00:14:56,120
This is the part where kind of the systems and sort of architecture co-design starts to come in.

178
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:00,710
Percy mentioned, you know, in the previous lecture, this idea of arithmetic intensity, right?

179
00:15:00,710 --> 00:15:07,150
We want to keep our GPUs hot by doing, you know, matrix multiplies and other very intense computations.

180
00:15:07,150 --> 00:15:12,750
We do not want to be wasting our GPUs by having them move little tiny bits of memory back and forth, right?

181
00:15:12,750 --> 00:15:16,040
That's a very inefficient use of our, you know, very powerful GPU.

182
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:24,910
And so, what we want is to remove operations that are small and involve memory movement, but don't give us much expressive power, right?

183
00:15:24,910 --> 00:15:34,390
So, by that view, what we really want to be doing here is, you know, if the mean subtraction and addition isn't really doing much for us, just get rid of it, right?

184
00:15:34,390 --> 00:15:38,190
you might think, "Okay, why does this matter?

185
00:15:38,190 --> 00:15:45,069
We're just optimizing this teeny, tiny operation that accounts for, you know, in this case, something like 0.

186
00:15:45,069 --> 00:15:49,120
17%of the total floating point operations of our system."

187
00:15:49,120 --> 00:15:53,550
But, you know, as Percy mentioned, it's not really about the flops, right?

188
00:15:53,550 --> 00:16:01,030
The flops are the the floating point operations we do, that's sort of multiplying matrices, but that's not runtime, right?

189
00:16:01,030 --> 00:16:03,630
Runtime is a much more complicated object.

190
00:16:03,630 --> 00:16:10,030
And, you know, statistical normalizations, things like layer norms, even though they're only 0.

191
00:16:10,030 --> 00:16:13,959
17% of the flops, depending on your workload and depending on the setup, can be up to 25% of the runtime, right?

192
00:16:13,959 --> 00:16:17,750
That's kind of crazy.

